
# Grids are 'N' (cubic) or 'WxHxD'. The three are in storage order, the order of the file
# and of the metadata's 'Grid size:', which is galactic (X, Z, -Y) - a flat disk, whose
# thickness is the galactic Z, wants the middle number small (e.g. 400x40x400).
#
# The box is normalized: its largest side is NORMALIZED_SIDE (1.37) in units of the object's
# size in the scene, so the object's sizePc decides how large the volume is drawn and the data
# only decides its shape. More than 1 draws the gas larger than sizePc - the galaxy uses it to
# agree with the reference render - while the label, the hit sphere and the go-to distance stay
# at sizePc. A voxel size of 1/400 therefore asks for 400 voxels along the largest axis.
# There is no switch for this: older volumes were written in raw source units, where the
# metadata bounds also acted as a size multiplier on the object (a box of side 7 drew the
# volume 7 times the declared size), so those must not be mixed with these.
#
# The density blur is a length in the world, not a count of voxels: by default 0.85 of the
# channel's *local* inter-particle spacing (the median distance from a particle to its
# 4th neighbour, DEFAULT_WIDTH in particles-to-volume.py). Local, not the mean spacing of
# the box: the box figure assumes the channel fills the box, and the dust does not - it is a
# thin disk in a few percent of it - so sizing by the box gave the dust a blur 3.5x thicker
# than the disk itself, which is what "puffy" is. Both figures are lengths on the particles, so
# the same particles drawn at 200 and at 800 get the same galaxy.
#
# One fraction cannot be right for every channel at once - a dense thin disk and a diffuse halo
# want different amounts - so --smoothing overrides it per channel, as a fraction of that
# channel's own spacing:
#
#     just genvol 400 <in> <out> --smoothing ArmDust=0.4
#
# To set the blur outright instead of as a multiple of the spacing, --width takes a length in
# box units - a fraction of the longest side of the box, so 1/400 is one voxel at 400 and the
# same number is the same length at every resolution:
#
#     just genvol 400 <in> <out> --width ArmDust=0.004
#
# The two compose: --smoothing multiplies whatever --width set.
#
# The resulting width, in world units and in this grid's voxels, is in each channel's metadata,
# alongside the two lengths it was sized and capped by - which is the cheap way to tell whether
# two runs were generated to look the same.
#
#
# VARIADIC ARGS ARE INTERPOLATED UNQUOTED. '{{args}}' splices the extra arguments into
# the command line as raw text, so anything the shell reads as syntax breaks: a group
# spec contains a '|', and `--group 'Gas+Bulge:0.6|ArmDust'` arrives at the recipe as
# `--group Gas+Bulge:0.6|ArmDust`, where the shell takes the '|' for a pipe ("ArmDust:
# command not found"). Recipes that take a group spec therefore take it as a *named*
# parameter and quote it themselves; for the others, escape the metacharacter
# (`--group Gas+Bulge:0.6\|ArmDust`), which the shell then hands over intact.

# Generate the emissive and occlusive galaxy volumes in one go, from the particle channels.
#
#     just genvol 400 <in> <out>
#     just genvol 400 <in> <out> --intensity Bulge=0.3
#     just genvol 400 <in> <out> --smoothing ArmDust=0.5
#
# 'resolution' is the number of voxels along the longest side of the box; the other axes follow
# the shape of the data, so the voxels stay cubic. Extra arguments go straight to the script, and
# there are three of them: --intensity NAME=FLOAT (0..1) dims one channel, --smoothing
# NAME=FACTOR (greater than 0, 1 = the default) makes it thinner or thicker, and --width
# NAME=LENGTH sets the blur as a length in box units instead of a multiple of the spacing. All
# three are per channel and all three are resolution independent. Nothing else is exposed here
# because nothing else is needed for the usual run. This writes <prefix>_emissive_rgba.raw.gz and
# <prefix>_occlusive_rgba.raw.gz, which are the two textures the scene's volume0/volume1 want.

# Run with user-defined smoothing and intensity
genvol resolution input output *args:
	uv run --with numpy --with scipy python particles-to-volume.py {{resolution}} {{input}} {{output}} {{args}}

# Run with default values smoothing and intensity
genvold resolution input output:
	uv run --with numpy --with scipy python particles-to-volume.py {{resolution}} {{input}} {{output}} --smoothing ArmDust=0.4 --intensity ArmDust=0.06 --intensity Bulge=0.2 --intensity Gas=0.7

# Create top-down and edge-on images
img dir *args:
	uv run --with numpy --with matplotlib volume-preview.py {{dir}} --gamma 1.5 --saturation 2.5 --exposure 15.0 {{args}}
